cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "A certified Evans-Coleman critical solution: existence, one growing spherical mode, and its exponent"
authors:
  - name: "Moki & Julio"
version: "1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24303877"
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - critical collapse
  - Evans-Coleman solution
  - radiation fluid
  - self-similar solutions
  - Einstein-Euler equations
  - critical exponent
  - linear stability
  - computer-assisted proof
  - interval arithmetic
  - rigorous numerics
abstract: >-
  The Evans-Coleman solution is the continuously self-similar critical solution for the spherical collapse of a
  radiation fluid (p = rho/3). We give an interval-arithmetic (computer-assisted) certificate that a solution with its
  defining properties exists (regular centre, analytic saddle sonic crossing, one zero of V, continuation to t = 0),
  with KHA sonic velocity V0 in [0.1124394013880983362 +- 3.31e-20], and a blind second proof by a different method whose
  enclosure agrees to 19 digits; local uniqueness is not claimed. On it, an exact gauge deflation, an argument-principle
  count on a half-disc of radius 40 and a certified a-priori bound for all |kappa| >= 40 show exactly one growing
  non-gauge spherical linear mode (analytic through the sonic point) in Re kappa >= 0, with kappa* in
  [2.810552549 +- 3.83e-10] and gamma = 1/kappa* in [0.3558019224 +- 6.97e-11]. A blind second computation (different gauge and code) on the B5 solution encloses a real growth rate in
  [2.8105525488272 +- 7.32e-14], inside the first (certified, beyond the sealed bar). If the two solutions coincide
  (not claimed), the value 2.8105525488 printed in the table of Hara, Koike and Adachi (1996) is correct to its last
  digit and two further digits are certified. Every sealed test is listed, including
  three FAILED and one VOID existence attempt.
